  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nTonight\n\nWith southeast winds ushering in drier air into the region \nand a well-mixed boundary layer underneath the nocturnal\ninversion, not expecting widespread fog tonight. If there is \nany, it would be found in the Wisconsin River, lower Kickapoo\nriver, and Mississippi river tributaries. \n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-50s to lower 60 in\nwestern Wisconsin, and in the lower and mid-60s in northeast\nIowa and southeast Minnesota.\n\nLabor Day\n\nA 1023 mb high pressure system centered over Michigan's Thumb\narea will keep much of the area dry on Labor Day. If there is\nany rain (up to 20 percent), it would be west of Highway 52 in\nnortheast Iowa and southeast Minnesota.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-70s to lower 80s. The\nwarmest temperatures will be found in the river valleys.\n\nLate Monday night into Wednesday morning\n\nA strong, shortwave trough will move slowly east from the \nNorthern Plains into the Great Lakes. Southerly winds will pool\nthe low-level moisture along the cold front as its moves slowly\nsoutheast through the Upper Mississippi River Valley from \nTuesday afternoon into Tuesday night. Precipitable water values \nwill range from 2 to to 2.5 inches and warm cloud depths will \nrange from 4 to 4.5 km. This will result in some heavy rain. The\n48-hour NBM-CONUS Whas a 40-50 percent chance of 1 inch or \ngreater and a 5 to 20 percent chance of 2 inches or more. \n\nSurface-based CAPES will range from 250 to 1250 J/kg along and\nahead of this front. The 0-3 km shear will range from 30 to 40\nknots. This could result in some strong to severe storms. The\nmain threats will be damaging winds and large hail on Tuesday\nafternoon and evening.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-70s to mid-80s on\nTuesday and from the mid-70s to lower 80s on Wednesday. Low\ntemperatures on Tuesday night will be in the 60s.",
